1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,314
Picture Sam that Tuesday: the clinical-authority cut, ex-ER nurse credentials and

2
00:00:05,314 --> 00:00:10,463
the spec first, against the raw cut with no framing at all, both live the same hour.

3
00:00:10,463 --> 00:00:12,424
He bet one would win everywhere.

4
00:00:12,424 --> 00:00:17,450
Meta answered first, and the clinical cut took it, clearly enough that Sam stopped

5
00:00:17,450 --> 00:00:17,818
there.

6
00:00:17,818 --> 00:00:19,472
Meta doesn't have one feed.

7
00:00:19,472 --> 00:00:23,885
It has Feed, Stories, and Reels, and each one crops, frames, and behaves

8
00:00:23,885 --> 00:00:24,621
differently.

9
00:00:24,621 --> 00:00:29,769
As of this recording, three ratios cover nearly all of Meta's delivery: four-by-five

10
00:00:29,769 --> 00:00:34,795
vertical for Feed, nine-by-sixteen for Stories and Reels, and one-by-one square as

11
00:00:34,795 --> 00:00:39,330
the safe fallback when you're not sure which placement will pick up an ad.

12
00:00:39,330 --> 00:00:44,172
Nine-by-sixteen has only grown more central since Instagram folded its separate

13
00:00:44,172 --> 00:00:49,198
Explore feed into Reels earlier this year, pushing more discovery traffic into the

14
00:00:49,198 --> 00:00:50,363
full-screen format.

15
00:00:50,363 --> 00:00:54,775
Confirm these specifics in your own ads manager before you rely on them.

16
00:00:54,775 --> 00:00:58,453
Meta reshapes its placements on its own schedule, not yours.

17
00:00:58,453 --> 00:01:01,272
Why does this matter more now than it used to?

18
00:01:01,272 --> 00:01:04,888
A landscape ad used to be close enough for every placement.

19
00:01:04,888 --> 00:01:10,037
That's no longer true: as more of Meta's own traffic moves into full-screen vertical

20
00:01:10,037 --> 00:01:14,940
formats, a landscape ad loses the top and bottom of frame to letterboxing on the

21
00:01:14,940 --> 00:01:17,330
placement most people now actually use.

22
00:01:17,330 --> 00:01:22,295
"Close enough" and "built for the format" have drifted further apart, not closer.

23
00:01:22,845 --> 00:01:24,876
Notice none of these is landscape.

24
00:01:24,876 --> 00:01:29,715
Every shape Meta actually rewards is taller than it is wide, because every one of

25
00:01:29,715 --> 00:01:34,255
them is designed to fill a phone held upright, not a screen turned sideways.

26
00:01:34,255 --> 00:01:38,676
This matters for the assembly discipline you already built in Section 4.5.

27
00:01:38,676 --> 00:01:43,515
If you cut your avatar and product footage in nine-by-sixteen from the start, one

28
00:01:43,515 --> 00:01:47,936
export covers Stories and Reels outright, and a centred crop covers Feed's

29
00:01:47,936 --> 00:01:50,206
four-by-five without losing the frame.

30
00:01:50,206 --> 00:01:55,045
Cut in landscape first, and you're fighting a crop on every single placement Meta

31
00:01:55,045 --> 00:01:56,419
actually delivers into.

32
00:01:56,419 --> 00:01:59,287
There's a practical order worth locking in here.

33
00:01:59,287 --> 00:02:04,185
Shoot and generate at nine-by-sixteen first, treat everything else as a derivative

34
00:02:04,185 --> 00:02:04,603
export.

35
00:02:04,603 --> 00:02:09,562
Reframing a square or landscape source up to full-screen usually means losing edges

36
00:02:09,562 --> 00:02:11,533
or padding them with empty space.

37
00:02:11,533 --> 00:02:16,253
Reframing full-screen down to a square or a four-by-five almost never costs you

38
00:02:16,253 --> 00:02:20,674
anything, because you're cropping into a taller canvas with room to spare.

39
00:02:20,674 --> 00:02:25,632
One more reason to get this right at generation time: Section 4.5's caption and CTA

40
00:02:25,632 --> 00:02:28,201
placement was tuned to a full-screen frame.

41
00:02:28,201 --> 00:02:32,801
Crop it down after the fact and captions can drift relative to the new edges,

42
00:02:32,801 --> 00:02:37,819
exactly the kind of gap a muted-watch test on the wrong export would catch too late.

43
00:02:38,369 --> 00:02:43,358
Here's the part that catches people who cut a full-screen ad without checking it

44
00:02:43,358 --> 00:02:45,541
against the actual interface first.

45
00:02:45,541 --> 00:02:49,657
The top and bottom of a Stories or Reels frame aren't fully yours.

46
00:02:49,657 --> 00:02:54,833
Meta's own profile bar, mute icon, caption line and CTA button sit over roughly the

47
00:02:54,833 --> 00:02:57,452
top and bottom seventh of the screen each.

48
00:02:57,452 --> 00:03:02,503
Put your hook text at the very top of frame, the way you might on a plain canvas,

49
00:03:02,503 --> 00:03:06,058
and the platform covers it before a viewer ever reads it.

50
00:03:06,058 --> 00:03:11,296
Check this the same way you checked a caption in Section 4.5: play the actual export

51
00:03:11,296 --> 00:03:16,036
inside the actual platform, not just inside your edit tool's preview window.

52
00:03:16,036 --> 00:03:20,900
An edit tool has no idea where Meta's interface is going to sit on top of your

53
00:03:20,900 --> 00:03:21,461
creative.

54
00:03:21,461 --> 00:03:23,270
Where does this bite hardest?

55
00:03:23,270 --> 00:03:23,768
The CTA.

56
00:03:23,768 --> 00:03:29,007
You spent Section 4.5 making sure "Harlan, link in bio" stayed on screen long enough

57
00:03:29,007 --> 00:03:29,506
to read.

58
00:03:29,506 --> 00:03:34,744
Put that same line flush against the bottom edge of a full-screen export, and Meta's

59
00:03:34,744 --> 00:03:39,608
own CTA button sits directly on top of it, unread, for the entire time it's on

60
00:03:39,608 --> 00:03:40,045
screen.

61
00:03:40,045 --> 00:03:42,040
The line was designed correctly.

62
00:03:42,040 --> 00:03:47,154
The frame it landed in wasn't checked against the platform that was actually going

63
00:03:47,154 --> 00:03:47,840
to show it.

64
00:03:48,390 --> 00:03:53,363
As of this recording, the pattern holds across most of what's published about Meta

65
00:03:53,363 --> 00:03:57,971
performance: creative that looks native to the feed it's sitting in tends to

66
00:03:57,971 --> 00:04:01,610
outperform creative that announces itself as an ad on sight.

67
00:04:01,610 --> 00:04:05,248
That's the exact case for everything you built in Section 4.

68
00:04:05,248 --> 00:04:09,129
An avatar-led, sound-off-designed ad isn't a compromise on Meta.

69
00:04:09,129 --> 00:04:12,101
It's closer to what the platform already rewards.

70
00:04:12,101 --> 00:04:15,315
The other half of that pattern is volume, not polish.

71
00:04:15,315 --> 00:04:20,409
Meta's own automated delivery tools, grouped under the Advantage+ name, are built to

72
00:04:20,409 --> 00:04:25,321
test a real spread of creative variants against each other and shift spend toward

73
00:04:25,321 --> 00:04:27,261
whichever ones actually perform.

74
00:04:27,261 --> 00:04:31,446
Feed them one hero ad and you've given the system nothing to compare.

75
00:04:31,446 --> 00:04:36,418
Feed them the eight to twenty variants a real angle map produces, and you've given

76
00:04:36,418 --> 00:04:38,601
it something to actually learn from.

77
00:04:38,601 --> 00:04:42,786
Notice how directly this connects back to Section 1.4's volume maths.

78
00:04:42,786 --> 00:04:46,849
Advantage+ isn't a separate skill from what you've already learned.

79
00:04:46,849 --> 00:04:51,821
It's the same argument, one video can't reveal a winner, applied inside Meta's own

80
00:04:51,821 --> 00:04:55,702
delivery system instead of inside your own testing architecture.

81
00:04:55,702 --> 00:04:59,098
The system doesn't reward the one ad you're proudest of.

82
00:04:59,098 --> 00:05:04,010
It rewards whichever one earns its keep against the others you gave it to compare

83
00:05:04,010 --> 00:05:04,495
against.

84
00:05:05,045 --> 00:05:07,828
One honest note before this goes any further.

85
00:05:07,828 --> 00:05:12,652
Everything in this lecture, the ratios, the safe zones, the variant counts, is

86
00:05:12,652 --> 00:05:15,311
accurate as checked at the time of writing.

87
00:05:15,311 --> 00:05:20,321
Meta reshapes its own placements more often than most advertisers notice, because

88
00:05:20,321 --> 00:05:22,361
most advertisers aren't checking.

89
00:05:22,361 --> 00:05:27,433
Five minutes in your own ads manager before a campaign launches costs you nothing.

90
00:05:27,433 --> 00:05:32,442
Finding out mid-flight that a safe zone moved costs you a whole batch of creative

91
00:05:32,442 --> 00:05:33,679
that's already live.

92
00:05:33,679 --> 00:05:38,750
Ask yourself the same question this course keeps asking about every tool it names:

93
00:05:38,750 --> 00:05:42,893
what would actually break if this specific detail changed tomorrow?

94
00:05:42,893 --> 00:05:47,717
A ratio shift breaks an export setting, fixable in minutes once you notice it.

95
00:05:47,717 --> 00:05:52,912
It doesn't break the underlying discipline, generate in full-screen first, check the

96
00:05:52,912 --> 00:05:57,983
actual platform before you trust a preview, give the delivery system a real spread

97
00:05:57,983 --> 00:05:58,478
to test.

98
00:05:58,478 --> 00:06:02,065
That discipline is what this lecture is actually teaching.

99
00:06:02,065 --> 00:06:04,724
The numbers are just today's version of it.

100
00:06:05,274 --> 00:06:10,536
Next lecture takes the same creative set to a platform where the rules aren't just

101
00:06:10,536 --> 00:06:11,627
different ratios.

102
00:06:11,627 --> 00:06:16,890
TikTok rewards a genuinely different kind of cut, and Harlan's own set is about to

103
00:06:16,890 --> 00:06:21,446
prove it: the cut that just lost on Meta is about to win there instead.

104
00:06:21,446 --> 00:06:26,580
Before that, open the actual video files behind your Section 4.6 brief, artifact

105
00:06:26,580 --> 00:06:30,944
B04, and sort them by which placement each one was actually cut for.

106
00:06:30,944 --> 00:06:36,014
If the honest answer for any of them is "none in particular", fix that tonight.

107
00:06:36,014 --> 00:06:41,020
It only gets harder to ignore once TikTok starts asking the same question in a

108
00:06:41,020 --> 00:06:42,175
different grammar.
